 ## Overview
-This method extracts watertight, crack-free isosurfaces from block-structured Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) data in a distributed-memory parallel environment [S1]. It solves the problem of T-junctions (cracks) that arise when standard Marching Cubes is applied to hierarchical data by using dual grids and ghost cells to ensure boundary consistency without requiring the entire dataset in a single processor's memory [S1]. Unlike [Octree-based Isosurfacing](1247-octree-based-isosurfacing.md) which operates on tree structures, this method operates on rectilinear boxes, and unlike [Grid Traversal and Data Structure Adaptation](8340-grid-traversal-and-data-structure-adaptation.md) which may adapt the grid structure itself, this method preserves the original AMR boxes while generating auxiliary "stitch cells" to bridge resolution gaps [S1].
+This method extracts watertight, crack-free isosurfaces from block-structured Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) data in a distributed-memory parallel environment [S1]. It solves the problem of T-junctions (cracks) that arise when standard Marching Cubes is applied to hierarchical data by using dual grids and ghost cells to ensure boundary consistency without requiring the entire dataset in a single processor's memory [S1]. Unlike [Octree-based Isosurfacing](1247-octree-based-isosurfacing.md) which operates on tree structures [G], this method operates on rectilinear boxes, and unlike [Grid Traversal and Data Structure Adaptation](8340-grid-traversal-and-data-structure-adaptation.md) which may adapt the grid structure itself [G], this method preserves the original AMR boxes while generating auxiliary "stitch cells" to bridge resolution gaps [S1].
 
